rlm-gcloud-venv [-f|--force]

Create (or verify) the dedicated Python virtualenv that the Google Cloud
SDK runs on, at ~/.local/gcloud-venv.

.zshenv exports CLOUDSDK_PYTHON and CLOUDSDK_PYTHON_SITEPACKAGES=1 only
when ~/.local/gcloud-venv/bin/python exists, so this function is the
bootstrap step on a fresh machine. It is deliberately not run from
.zshenv: that file is sourced by every non-interactive shell (make,
scripts, editor subshells) and a pip install must never fire from one
of those.

Pinning the interpreter matters because without CLOUDSDK_PYTHON gcloud
runs its own order_python() search over $PATH. With pyenv installed the
resolved interpreter then changes depending on the current directory's
.python-version, and gcloud re-resolves its dependencies against
whatever the shim happens to point at.

The venv is built from /usr/local/bin/python3.13 (the python.org
framework build) and carries grpcio and google-cloud-logging, which
CLOUDSDK_PYTHON_SITEPACKAGES=1 makes importable by gcloud.

Idempotent. When the venv already exists the required packages are
checked with "pip show" and installed only if absent; an already
complete venv is left untouched.

Options:

  -f, --force   Remove an existing venv and recreate it from scratch.
  -h, --help    Show this help.

After a first-time creation, open a new shell (or re-source ~/.zshenv)
so CLOUDSDK_PYTHON is exported.

Requires: /usr/local/bin/python3.13.

See also: rlm-glogin.
